  "query_preview": "..\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW:\nEastern Montana will be in SW flow aloft ahead of an upper low\nmoving into western Montana this morning and across Central\nMontana into SW Saskatchewan this afternoon. A humid airmass will\ncombine with the moist SW flow aloft keep an unstable atmosphere\nover the forecast area which will allow for showers and\nthunderstorms at times. A few storms early this morning could\nbecome severe with large hail and damaging winds. PWAT on GGW 00z\nsounding was 1.46 inches which is a record high for September.\nThis will provide stronger storms ample moisture for high rainfall\nrates especially this morning. As the low moves into Saskatchewan\nthis afternoon and evening, a dry slot will move north across NE\nMontana gradually diminishing the rain from south to north.\nMeanwhile, the surface low will move north through NE Montana\nalong MT 13 this morning and into Saskatchewan this afternoon \nwhere it will become stacked with the upper low. Strong west winds\nwill develop across the forecast area this afternoon and continue\ntonight and Tuesday with gusts to 40 to 50 mph possible. A Lake \nWind Advisory is in effect from noon today through midnight \nTuesday night. Wrap around rain is possible at times tonight and \nTuesday over the NW half of the forecast area while the SE will be\ndry most of the time. \n\nThe system exits the region Tuesday night and Wednesday and\nThursday are expected to be dry. \n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS:\n\nHigh confidence on rain this morning with diminishing confidence\nfor rain this afternoon. High confidence for strong west winds\nthis afternoon, tonight and Tuesday. Tweaked pops early this \nmorning. No other changes at this time.",
